<h2>How to Choose</h2>
<p><strong>If you are a team of one:</strong> start with Surfer's Discovery plan or Semrush's free tier for research, use a general AI assistant for drafting, and add ElevenLabs at {ELEVENLABS_PRICING.starter.price} the moment you need narrated video. That is under $60 a month and covers most of the output a solo marketer produces.</p>
<p><strong>If brand consistency is the problem:</strong> Jasper's brand voice system is the specific fix, and it is the reason the tool costs more than a general assistant. Budget Pro's $69 and skip the add-ons until you have a second writer.</p>
<p><strong>If you publish video:</strong> Synthesia for the presenter video and ElevenLabs if you want a narration voice that is not the avatar's. Both have free tiers generous enough to produce a pilot before you pay.</p>
<p><strong>If you already have an archive:</strong> the bottleneck is distribution, not production. Submagic's Starter plan converts existing footage into shorts; the credit maths matter more than the headline price, because Magic Clips cost more credits than plain captions.</p>
<p><strong>If you are choosing a CRM:</strong> the decision is mostly about how much automation you actually need. HubSpot Professional is a real jump in cost and comes with an onboarding fee — validate the workflow on Starter or the free tier first.</p>
<p><strong>Before subscribing to anything:</strong> convert the plan into finished output using the vendor's own conversion table. One character equals roughly one ElevenLabs credit; Synthesia converts at about 100 credits per finished minute; Submagic spends credits per video and more per clip. That single step prevents most "cheap plan, expensive month" surprises.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>What is the best AI tool for marketing copy in 2026?</h3>
<p>Jasper is the most marketing-specific option: Pro is $69/month ($59/month billed yearly) with a 7-day free trial, 100+ marketing agents, two brand voices and five knowledge assets. If your bottleneck is search-optimised content rather than brand-voice copywriting, Surfer at $49/month on Discovery is the better fit.</p>

<h3>How much do marketers pay per month for an AI stack?</h3>
<p>A sensible solo stack runs $80-150 a month: $49-69 for the content or SEO platform, $0-19 for avatar video, $0-6 for voiceover, $0-20 for automation. Teams adding Semrush at $139/month and HubSpot Professional at $890/month monthly ($800/month annual plus a one-time $3,000 onboarding fee) are into four figures, so most teams start with one research platform and add point tools.</p>

<h3>Do I need a separate AI tool for short-form video?</h3>
<p>If you already have long footage, yes. Submagic's Starter plan is {SUBMAGIC_PRICING.starter.price} ({SUBMAGIC_PRICING.starter.annual} billed yearly) for {SUBMAGIC_PRICING.starter.creditsNum} credits — about {SUBMAGIC_PRICING.starter.videos} captioned videos or 5 Magic Clips — with a free trial that needs no credit card. If you are producing short-form from scratch instead, Synthesia at {SYNTHESIA_PRICING.starter.price} covers avatar-led explainers.</p>

<h3>Is AI-generated marketing content bad for SEO?</h3>
<p>Google's guidance targets unhelpful scaled content, not automation itself. What gets sites filtered is publishing pages with no original information and no reason to exist beyond the keyword. The practical rule is AI drafting plus human verification of every number, claim and quote — which is why any page quoting vendor pricing should show the date it was checked.</p>

<h3>What is the cheapest way to test AI marketing tools?</h3>
<p>Stack the free tiers instead of subscribing to everything. Synthesia's Basic plan gives about 10 minutes of watermarked video a month, ElevenLabs gives 10,000 characters, HubSpot's free tier covers two users, Jasper runs a 7-day Pro trial and Submagic's trial covers a few videos without a card. Run one campaign end to end before committing to an annual plan.</p>

<h2>How We Evaluate</h2>
<p>This page is a buyer's guide, not a lab report: tools are compared on documented features, official pricing and published limits. We do not present lab-style test results we did not produce.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scope:</strong> The five jobs a marketing team repeats — research, copy, creative and video, distribution, and automation.</li>
<li><strong>Method:</strong> Feature lists, official pricing pages and vendor-published credit conversions, checked on the date shown above.</li>
<li><strong>Criteria:</strong> Free-tier usefulness, cost per finished output, seat and contact maths, and how much work the tool removes from a weekly cycle.</li>
<li><strong>Corrections:</strong> Prices move. If a figure here disagrees with the vendor's page, email admin@aizest.net and we will fix it.</li>
</ul>

